cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "lexindex: compact, immutable string-to-id indexes for huge catalogs"
abstract: >-
  Compact, immutable string-to-id indexes for huge catalogs, with a Rust core, Python
  bindings and a C ABI. Six complementary build-once / query-many structures: an ordered
  FST-backed index with prefix, range, fuzzy (Levenshtein) and subsequence queries; an
  ordered front-coded dictionary storing every key, which answers rank, lower-bound, prefix
  and range (2.64 bytes/key on real dictionary words); the same dictionary with a hash
  sidecar, which answers its ranks from a minimal perfect hash rather than a search; a
  minimal-perfect-hash dictionary with verified membership and reverse lookup; a
  fingerprint-based minimal-perfect-hash map storing no keys at all (1.24 bytes/key); and
  the bare perfect hash for a vocabulary known to be closed (0.24). A workload-aware planner
  prices five of them from a sample of the keys before one is built. All serialise to flat
  blobs, and every one but the bare perfect hash can be memory-mapped and borrowed rather
  than read.
